$D7E0 FontBase ; GlyphBase of the font: glyph n at $D7E0 + 8n, so codes $00-$1F would fall on this layout; the glyphs start at Font $D8E0 (code $20)

@module graphics ; The font, the scenery glyphs, the sprites
$D8E0 b Font 8 ; The font: 59 glyphs of 8 bytes, codes $20-$5A (space to Z), one glyph a row
> GlyphBase FontBase $D7E0 (set at $715D, $8E1F, $8E71, $A8DB, PrintNumber3 $B0C8, PrintString $E7 at $AFF5): code n at $D7E0 + 8n. Thick 7-pixel letters and digits (glyphs.py). In the runs PrintChar and PrintCharAttr read the space, !, the digits, : and the letters.
$DAB8 b TileGlyphs 8 ; The scenery glyphs: 8 bytes each, one glyph a row, in 23 sets (GlyphBase values)
> The objects of ObjectTable $852E select a set with PrintString $E6 lo hi and then print codes from 0: glyph n of a set at its base + 8n. A base is any address, the sets follow each other. The code sets three bases itself: $DAB8 (Mines, $AB9F, $AC2B), $DDD8 (AnimateTurrets, for the XOR plotter XorCharAttr), $E840 ($A2D5). strings.py lists for each base the objects that use it and the codes they print; the runs read $DAB8-$ED3F as glyphs (PrintChar, EraseBlockDebris, EraseBlock, XorCharAttr), with unread holes at $DBC0-$DBDF and $E5C0-$E5FF.
$DAB8 ; set $DAB8: objects 0, 1, 2, 12, and the code at $9152, $AB9F, $AC2B
$DBE8 TileSetDBE8 ; the glyph set of objects 2
$DD38 TileSetDD38 ; the glyph set of objects 3, 4, 35, 37
$DDD8 TileSetDDD8 ; the glyph set of objects 5, 44
$DED8 TileSetDED8 ; the glyph set of objects 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 14, 41, 56, 57, 58, 59, 60, 61
$DF78 TileSetDF78 ; the glyph set of objects 11, 25
$E018 TileSetE018 ; the glyph set of objects 13
$E110 TileSetE110 ; the glyph set of objects 15, 16
$E260 TileSetE260 ; the glyph set of objects 17
