$ED40 b SmallSprites 2 ; 9 small XOR sprites, 16 x 8 pixels, each in 4 pre-shifted copies: 64 bytes a sprite
> DrawSmallSprite: A the sprite, D the pixel line, E the X (2-pixel units); the copy (E AND 3) at $ED40 + 64A + 16 (E AND 3), 8 rows of 2 bytes XORed onto the screen at PixelAddress (POPs through SP, BC = $0407: 4 passes of 2 rows). Drawn twice it is gone. Each copy is shifted 2 pixels right of the one before (bitmap.py).
$ED40 SmallSprite0 ; an arrowhead pointing right
$ED80 SmallSprite1 ; the same pointing left
$EDC0 SmallSprite2 ; a spark (the debris particle: $9537, A = 2)
$EE00 SmallSprite3 ; a cluster of dots
$EE40 SmallSprite4 ; a smaller cluster
$EE80 SmallSprite5 ; three dots
$EEC0 SmallSprite6 ; blank
$EF00 SmallSprite7 ; a round blob, 8 x 8 (drawn at $9DB8 with A = 8 before PlaySfxNoiseSweep)
$EF40 SmallSprite8 ; a short bar

$EF80 b PlayerFrames 3 ; The player's 25 frames, 24 x 32 pixels: 32 rows of 3 bytes, 96 bytes a frame
> DrawBigSprite: A the frame, the rows from $EF80 + 96A (POPs through SP), shifted at drawing by (E * 2) AND 7 pixels through the shift table $78CA, the screen under it saved in the caller's buffer (the player's at WorkArea $B181) unless that is NoSaveBuffer. Frames 0-23: the soldier in his suit, 24 poses. FlipPlayerFrames mirrors exactly those 24 in place (768 rows: the outer bytes swapped, each byte bit-reversed through the table at $6400) and toggles $7BDA, so the frames face the way the player walks. Frame 24 ($F880, not mirrored) is no soldier: a bar on a post over a striped block (bitmap.py).
$EF80 ; frame 0
$EFE0 ; frame 1
$F040 ; frame 2
$F0A0 ; frame 3
$F100 ; frame 4
$F160 ; frame 5
$F1C0 ; frame 6
$F220 ; frame 7
$F280 ; frame 8
$F2E0 ; frame 9
$F340 ; frame 10
$F3A0 ; frame 11
$F400 ; frame 12
$F460 ; frame 13
$F4C0 ; frame 14
$F520 ; frame 15
$F580 ; frame 16
$F5E0 ; frame 17
$F640 ; frame 18
$F6A0 ; frame 19
$F700 ; frame 20
$F760 ; frame 21
$F7C0 ; frame 22
$F820 ; frame 23
$F880 ; frame 24

$F8E0 b Sprites16 2 ; 45 sprites of 16 x 16 pixels: 16 rows of 2 bytes, 32 bytes each
> DrawSprite: A the sprite, the rows from $F8E0 + 32A (POPs through SP), shifted at drawing by (E * 2) AND 7 through the table $7AD9. The shapes (bitmap.py): 0 blank; 1-4 a round body with a moving dot; 5-10 a ring of dots thinning out; 11 a wedge with a trail; 12-15 a cross growing rays; 16-19 a disc turning edge-on; 20-23 a striped capsule in four phases; 24-27 and 37-44 a horizontal launcher shape in phases; 28-36 thin arcs and lines (fragments). Which enemy uses which is for the routines that pass A.
$FE80 s SpriteSpare ; 152 zero bytes after sprite 44 (no reader)


@module leftover ; What the development machine left: the BASIC stack, the UDGs, a serial downloader
$FF18 b LeftoverStack 16 ; Leftover of the development machine: its BASIC machine stack
> Words such as $0DF3, $0BCE, $171E, $0ADC (48K ROM addresses), $50E2, $6D5F, and at $FF54 $1303 (the address the 48K ROM keeps at the bottom of every BASIC stack, the error return to MAIN-4), then $3E at $FF57: the marker byte the ROM puts at RAMTOP, here $FF57, the 48K default. The image was saved from a 48K whose BASIC stack and user-defined graphics lay at the top of RAM. No reader in any recording.
$FF58 LeftoverUdgs ; $FF58-$FF77: the user-defined graphics A-D as the ROM sets them (copies of the ROM font's A, B, C, D)

$FF78 c SerialDownloader ; A development downloader over the ZX Interface 1 RS232 port, left in the image (never ran)
> DI, the checksum SerialSum $FFDB = 0, then blocks: a count byte (0 ends), the address high and low, the bytes to (HL) (the border shows each byte's low 3 bits). After the 0 one more byte is compared with the checksum of everything received: a mismatch is JP $0000 (a reset), else EI and RET. Bytes come from SerialReadByte $FFA7. It lies over the UDGs E-P; no code of the game reaches it and no recording ran it: the transfer tool of the cross-development setup the code was sent with.
$FF7D SerialDownloaderBlock ; the next block: its count, 0 the end
$FF8C SerialDownloaderByte ; a byte to (HL), the border from it
$FF9A SerialDownloaderCheck ; the checksum byte
$FFA7 SerialReadByte ; one byte off the Interface 1 RS232 line: CTS on (port $EF = $FE), the start bit awaited on port $F7 bit 7, 8 bits about 9600 baud apart (DJNZ $23 to the middle, $19 a bit), CTS off ($EE); A = the byte (the line inverted), XORed into SerialSum
$FFC0 SerialReadBit
$FFDB b SerialSum 1 ; The downloader's checksum
$FFDC LeftoverCode ; $FFDC-$FFFA: an older copy of SerialReadByte's tail (the bytes of $FFB3-$FFD1, then CPL and RET: no checksum); $FFFB-$FFFF the end of UDG U

# The lead's names for the jump targets the fragments left unnamed
$A29F HomingMissileYStep ; D one up (or one down after the two DECs)
$A570 AlienFindFree ; one 10-byte alien record a turn
$A95A BonusScreenAdd ; 1000 points a time, B times
$AF66 PrintInkSet ; the ink code as an attribute (bright for 8-15)
$B813 TuneBendAdd ; the pitch offset added to the period
$B815 TunePeriodOut ; the period to the channel's two AY registers
$B831 TuneVolumeFloor ; the volume kept at 0 or more
$B83F TuneVolumeCeiling ; the volume kept to the ceiling
$B899 TuneChannelReads ; this channel's turn to read the stream
$B8AA AyTuneNotePeriod ; the note's period from NotePeriods
$B921 TuneNextCode ; on to the next stream code
$B9C7 TuneCopyBytes ; the call's bytes into the channel's record
$C035 SfxNotePeriod ; the note's period from NotePeriods
$FFBC SerialStartWait ; half a bit's time
$FFC5 SerialBitWait ; one bit's time
